--\n38 |     X : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_dim)\n39 | \n40 |     Y : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_dim)\n41 | \n42 |     indices : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_dim)\n43 |             Indices of the points in Y used to compute the barycenter\n44 | \n45 |     reg : float, default=1e-3\n46 |         Amount of regularization to add for the problem to be\n47 |         well-posed in the case of n_neighbors > n_dim\n48 | \n49 |     Returns\n50 |     -------\n51 |     B : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_neighbors)\n52 | \n53 |     Notes\n54 |     -----\n55 |     See developers note for more information.\n56 |     \"\"\"\n57 |     X = check_array(X, dtype=FLOAT_DTYPES)\n58 |     Y = check_array(Y, dtype=FLOAT_DTYPES)\n59 |     indices = check_array(indices, dtype=int)\n60 | \n61 |     n_samples, n_neighbors = indices.shape\n62 |     assert X.shape[0] == n_samples\n63 | \n64 |     B = np.empty((n_samples, n_neighbors), dtype=X.dtype)\n65 |     v = np.ones(n_neighbors, dtype=X.dtype)\n66 | \n67 |     # this might raise a LinalgError if G is singular and has trace\n68 |     # zero\n69 |     for i, ind in enumerate(indices):\n70 |         A = Y[ind]\n71 |         C = A - X[i]  # broadcasting\n72 |         G = np.dot(C, C.T)\n73 |         trace = np.trace(G)\n74 |         if trace > 0:\n75 |             R = reg * trace\n76 |         else:\n77 |             R = reg\n78 |         G.flat[:: n_neighbors + 1] += R\n79 |         w = solve(G, v, assume_a=\"pos\")\n80 |         B[i, :] = w / np.sum(w)\n81 |     return B\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_asarray_with_order\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_asarray_with_order`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_asarray_with_order`.\n```\n360 | def _asarray_with_order(array, dtype=None, order=None, copy=None, *, xp=None):\n361 |     \"\"\"Helper to support the order kwarg only for NumPy-backed arrays\n362 | \n363 |     Memory layout parameter `order` is not exposed in the Array API standard,\n364 |     however some input validation code in scikit-learn needs to work both\n365 |     for classes and functions that will leverage Array API only operations\n366 |     and for code that inherently relies on NumPy backed data containers with\n367 |     specific memory layout constraints (e.g. our own Cython code). The\n368 |     purpose of this helper is to make it possible to share code for data\n369 |     container validation without memory copies for both downstream use cases:\n370 |     the `order` parameter is only enforced if the input array implementation\n371 |     is NumPy based, otherwise `order` is just silently ignored.\n372 |     \"\"\"\n373 |     if xp is None:\n374 |         xp, _ = get_namespace(array)\n375 |     if _is_numpy_namespace(xp):\n376 |         # Use NumPy API to support order\n377 |         if copy is True:\n378 |             array = numpy.array(array, order=order, dtype=dtype)\n379 |         else:\n380 |             array = numpy.asarray(array, order=order, dtype=dtype)\n381 | \n382 |         # At this point array is a NumPy ndarray. We convert it to an array\n383 |         # container that is consistent with the input's namespace.\n384 |         return xp.asarray(array)\n385 |     else:\n386 |         return xp.asarray(array, dtype=dtype, copy=copy)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of the feature request and the codebase, I can now identify the key locations that need to be modified to implement sparse matrix output support in SplineTransformer. Here are the relevant locations:\n\n```\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 693-702\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: __init__\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 916-922\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: fit\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 943-950\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: transform\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 651-680\nfunction: check_array\n\nsklearn/utils/_array_api.py\nline: 360-386\nfunction: _asarray_with_