ltioutput'}\n 82 |         The type of the true target data, as output by\n 83 |         'utils.multiclass.type_of_target'.\n 84 | \n 85 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_outputs)\n 86 |         Ground truth (correct) target values.\n 87 | \n 88 |     y_pred : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_outputs)\n 89 |         Estimated target values.\n 90 | \n 91 |     multioutput : array-like of shape (n_outputs) or string in ['raw_values',\n 92 |         uniform_average', 'variance_weighted'] or None\n 93 |         Custom output weights if ``multioutput`` is array-like or\n 94 |         just the corresponding argument if ``multioutput`` is a\n 95 |         correct keyword.\n 96 |     \"\"\"\n 97 |     xp, _ = get_namespace(y_true, y_pred, multioutput, xp=xp)\n 98 | \n 99 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_pred)\n100 |     y_true = check_array(y_true, ensure_2d=False, dtype=dtype)\n101 |     y_pred = check_array(y_pred, ensure_2d=False, dtype=dtype)\n102 | \n103 |     if y_true.ndim == 1:\n104 |         y_true = xp.reshape(y_true, (-1, 1))\n105 | \n106 |     if y_pred.ndim == 1:\n107 |         y_pred = xp.reshape(y_pred, (-1, 1))\n108 | \n109 |     if y_true.shape[1] != y_pred.shape[1]:\n110 |         raise ValueError(\n111 |             \"y_true and y_pred have different number of output ({0}!={1})\".format(\n112 |                 y_true.shape[1], y_pred.shape[1]\n113 |             )\n114 |         )\n115 | \n116 |     n_outputs = y_true.shape[1]\n117 |     allowed_multioutput_str = (\"raw_values\", \"uniform_average\", \"variance_weighted\")\n118 |     if isinstance(multioutput, str):\n119 |         if multioutput not in allowed_multioutput_str:\n120 |             raise ValueError(\n121 |                 \"Allowed 'multioutput' string values are {}. \"\n122 |                 \"You provided multioutput={!r}\".format(\n123 |                     allowed_multioutput_str, multioutput\n124 |                 )\n125 |             )\n126 |     elif multioutput is not None:\n127 |         multioutput = check_array(multioutput, ensure_2d=False)\n128 |         if n_outputs == 1:\n129 |             raise ValueError(\"Custom weights are useful only in multi-output cases.\")\n130 |         elif n_outputs != multioutput.shape[0]:\n131 |             raise ValueError(\n132 |                 \"There must be equally many custom weights \"\n133 |                 f\"({multioutput.shape[0]}) as outputs ({n_outputs}).\"\n134 |             )\n135 |     y_type = \"continuous\" if n_outputs == 1 else \"continuous-multioutput\"\n136 | \n137 |     return y_type, y_true, y_pred, multioutput\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_find_matching_floating_dtype\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_find_matching_floating_dtype`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_find_matching_floating_dtype`.\n```\n673 | def _find_matching_floating_dtype(*arrays, xp):\n674 |     \"\"\"Find a suitable floating point dtype when computing with arrays.\n675 | \n676 |     If any of the arrays are floating point, return the dtype with the highest\n677 |     precision by following official type promotion rules:\n678 | \n679 |     https://data-apis.org/array-api/latest/API_specification/type_promotion.html\n680 | \n681 |     If there are no floating point input arrays (all integral inputs for\n682 |     instance), return the default floating point dtype for the namespace.\n683 |     \"\"\"\n684 |     dtyped_arrays = [a for a in arrays if hasattr(a, \"dtype\")]\n685 |     floating_dtypes = [\n686 |         a.dtype for a in dtyped_arrays if xp.isdtype(a.dtype, \"real floating\")\n687 |     ]\n688 |     if floating_dtypes:\n689 |         # Return the floating dtype with the highest precision:\n690 |         return xp.result_type(*floating_dtypes)\n691 | \n692 |     # If none of the input arrays have a floating point dtype, they must be all\n693 |     # integer arrays or containers of Python scalars: return the default\n694 |     # floating point dtype for the namespace (implementation specific).\n695 |     return xp.asarray(0.0).dtype\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"get_namespace\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:get_namespace`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `get_namespace`.\n```\n490 | def get_namespace(*arrays, remove_none=True, remove_types=(str,), xp=None):\n491 |     \"\"\"Get namespace of arrays.\n492 | \n493 |     Introspect `arrays` arguments and return their common Array API compatible\n494 |     namespace object, if any.\n495 | \n496 |     Note that sparse arrays are filtered by default.\n497 | \n498 |     See: https://numpy.org/neps/nep-0047-array-api-standard.html\n499 | \n500 |     If `arrays` are regular numpy arrays, an instance of the `_NumPyAPIWrapper`\n501 |     compatibility wrapper is returned instead.\n502 | \n503 |     Namespace support is not enabled by default. To enabled it call:\n504 | \n505 |       sklearn.set_config(array_api_dispatch=True)\n506 | \n507 |     or:\n508 | \n509 |       with sklearn.config_context(array_api_dispatch=True):\n510 |           # your code here\n511 | \n512 |     Otherwise an instance of the `_NumPyAPIWrapper` compatibility wrapper is\n513 |     always returned irrespective of the fact that arrays implement the\n514 |     `__array_namespace__` protocol or not.\n515 | \n516 |     Note that if no arrays pass the set filters, ``_NUMPY_API_WRAPPER_INSTANCE, False``\n517 |     is returned.\n518 | \n519 |     Parameters\n520 |     ----------\n521 |     *arrays : array objects\n522 |         Array objects.\n523 | \n524 |     remove_none : bool, default=True\n525 |         Whether to ignore None objects passed in arrays.\n526 | \n527 |     remove_types : tuple or list, default=(str,)\n528 |         Types to ignore in the arrays.\n529 | \n530 |     xp : module, default=None\n531 |         Precomputed array namespace module. When passed, typically from a caller\n532 |         that has already performed inspection of its own inputs, skips array\n533 |         namespace inspection.\n534 | \n535 |     Returns\n536 |     -------\n537 |     namespace : module\n538 |         Namespace shared by array objects. If any of the `arrays` are not arrays,\n539 |         the namespace defaults to NumPy.\n540 | \n541 |     is_array_api_compliant : bool\n542 |         True if the arrays are containers that implement the Array API spec.\n543 |         Always False when array_api_dispatch=False.\n544 |     \"\"\"\n545 |     array_api_dispatch = get_config()[\"array_api_dispatch\"]\n546 |     if not array_api_dispatch:\n547 |         if xp is not None:\n548 |             return xp, False\n549 |         else:\n550 |             return _NUMPY_API_WRAPPER_INSTANCE, False\n551 | \n552 |     if xp is not None:\n553 |         return xp, True\n554 | \n555 |     arrays = _remove_non_arrays(\n556 |         *arrays,\n557 |         remove_none=remove_none,\n558 |         remove_types=remove_types,\n559 |     )\n560 | \n561 |     if not arrays:\n562 |         return _NUMPY_API_WRAPPER_INSTANCE, False\n563 | \n564 |     _check_array_api_dispatch(array_api_dispatch)\n565 | \n566 |     # array-api-compat is a required dependency of scikit-learn only when\n567 |     # configuring `array_api_dispatch=True`. Its import should therefore be\n568 |     # protected by _check_array_api_dispatch to display an informative error\n569 |     # message in case it is missing.\n570 |     import array_api_compat\n571 | \n572 |     namespace, is_array_api_compliant = array_api_compat.get_namespace(*arrays), True\n573 | \n574 |     if namespace.__name__ == \"array_api_strict\" and hasattr(\n575 |         namespace, \"set_array_api_strict_flags\"\n576 |     ):\n577 |         namespace.set_array_api_strict_flags(api_version=\"2023.12\")\n578 | \n579 |     return namespace, is_array_api_compliant\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me explore the dependencies around these functions to better understand the execution flow.\n\n<function=ex