ataFrame.rename : Alter DataFrame index labels or name.\n1161 |         Index.rename : Set new names on index.\n1162 | \n1163 |         Notes\n1164 |         -----\n1165 |         ``DataFrame.rename_axis`` supports two calling conventions\n1166 | \n1167 |         * ``(index=index_mapper, columns=columns_mapper, ...)``\n1168 |         * ``(mapper, axis={'index', 'columns'}, ...)``\n1169 | \n1170 |         The first calling convention will only modify the names of\n1171 |         the index and/or the names of the Index object that is the columns.\n1172 |         In this case, the parameter ``copy`` is ignored.\n1173 | \n1174 |         The second calling convention will modify the names of the\n1175 |         corresponding index if mapper is a list or a scalar.\n1176 |         However, if mapper is dict-like or a function, it will use the\n1177 |         deprecated behavior of modifying the axis *labels*.\n1178 | \n1179 |         We *highly* recommend using keyword arguments to clarify your\n1180 |         intent.\n1181 | \n1182 |         Examples\n1183 |         --------\n1184 |         **DataFrame**\n1185 | \n1186 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1187 |         ...     {\"num_legs\": [4, 4, 2], \"num_arms\": [0, 0, 2]}, [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]\n1188 |         ... )\n1189 |         >>> df\n1190 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1191 |         dog            4         0\n1192 |         cat            4         0\n1193 |         monkey         2         2\n1194 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"animal\")\n1195 |         >>> df\n1196 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1197 |         animal\n1198 |         dog            4         0\n1199 |         cat            4         0\n1200 |         monkey         2         2\n1201 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"limbs\", axis=\"columns\")\n1202 |         >>> df\n1203 |         limbs   num_legs  num_arms\n1204 |         animal\n1205 |         dog            4         0\n1206 |         cat            4         0\n1207 |         monkey         2         2\n1208 | \n1209 |         **MultiIndex**\n1210 | \n1211 |         >>> df.index = pd.MultiIndex.from_product(\n1212 |         ...     [[\"mammal\"], [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]], names=[\"type\", \"name\"]\n1213 |         ... )\n1214 |         >>> df\n1215 |         limbs          num_legs  num_arms\n1216 |         type   name\n1217 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1218 |                cat            4         0\n1219 |                monkey         2         2\n1220 | \n1221 |         >>> df.rename_axis(index={\"type\": \"class\"})\n1222 |         limbs          num_legs  num_arms\n1223 |         class  name\n1224 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1225 |                cat            4         0\n1226 |                monkey         2         2\n1227 | \n1228 |         >>> df.rename_axis(columns=str.upper)\n1229 |         LIMBS          num_legs  num_arms\n1230 |         type   name\n1231 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1232 |                cat            4         0\n1233 |                monkey         2         2\n1234 |         \"\"\"\n1235 |         self._check_copy_deprecation(copy)\n1236 |         axes = {\"index\": index, \"columns\": columns}\n1237 | \n1238 |         if axis is not None:\n1239 |             axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n1240 | \n1241 |         inplace = validate_bool_kwarg(inplace, \"inplace\")\n1242 | \n1243 |         if mapper is not lib.no_default:\n1244 |             # Use v0.23 behavior if a scalar or list\n1245 |             non_mapper = is_scalar(mapper) or (\n1246 |                 is_list_like(mapper) and not is_dict_like(mapper)\n1247 |             )\n1248 |             if non_mapper:\n1249 |                 return self._set_axis_name(mapper, axis=axis, inplace=inplace)\n1250 |             else:\n1251 |                 raise ValueError(\"Use `.rename` to alter labels with a mapper.\")\n1252 |         else:\n1253 |             # Use new behav