|         return newb\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/groupby/generic.py:DataFrameGroupBy.filter`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2259 | def filter(self, func, dropna: bool = True, *args, **kwargs) -> DataFrame:\n2260 |         \"\"\"\n2261 |         Filter elements from groups that don't satisfy a criterion.\n2262 | \n2263 |         Elements from groups are filtered if they do not satisfy the\n2264 |         boolean criterion specified by func.\n2265 | \n2266 |         Parameters\n2267 |         ----------\n2268 |         func : function\n2269 |             Criterion to apply to each group. Should return True or False.\n2270 |         dropna : bool\n2271 |             Drop groups that do not pass the filter. True by default; if False,\n2272 |             groups that evaluate False are filled with NaNs.\n2273 |         *args : tuple\n2274 |             Additional positional arguments to pass to `func`.\n2275 |         **kwargs : dict\n2276 |             Additional keyword arguments to pass to `func`.\n2277 | \n2278 |         Returns\n2279 |         -------\n2280 |         DataFrame\n2281 |             The filtered subset of the original DataFrame.\n2282 | \n2283 |         See Also\n2284 |         --------\n2285 |         DataFrame.filter: Filter elements of ungrouped DataFrame.\n2286 |         SeriesGroupBy.filter : Filter elements from groups base on criterion.\n2287 | \n2288 |         Notes\n2289 |         -----\n2290 |         Each subframe is endowed the attribute 'name' in case you need to know\n2291 |         which group you are working on.\n2292 | \n2293 |         Functions that mutate the passed object can produce unexpected\n2294 |         behavior or errors and are not supported. See :ref:`gotchas.udf-mutation`\n2295 |         for more details.\n2296 | \n2297 |         Examples\n2298 |         --------\n2299 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n2300 |         ...     {\n2301 |         ...         \"A\": [\"foo\", \"bar\", \"foo\", \"bar\", \"foo\", \"bar\"],\n2302 |         ...         \"B\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6],\n2303 |         ...         \"C\": [2.0, 5.0, 8.0, 1.0, 2.0, 9.0],\n2304 |         ...     }\n2305 |         ... )\n2306 |         >>> grouped = df.groupby(\"A\")\n2307 |         >>> grouped.filter(lambda x: x[\"B\"].mean() > 3.0)\n2308 |              A  B    C\n2309 |         1  bar  2  5.0\n2310 |         3  bar  4  1.0\n2311 |         5  bar  6  9.0\n2312 |         \"\"\"\n2313 |         indices = []\n2314 | \n2315 |         obj = self._selected_obj\n2316 |         gen = self._grouper.get_iterator(obj)\n2317 | \n2318 |         for name, group in gen:\n2319 |             # 2023-02-27 no tests are broken this pinning, but it is documented in the\n2320 |             #  docstring above.\n2321 |             object.__setattr__(group, \"name\", name)\n2322 | \n2323 |             res = func(group, *args, **kwargs)\n2324 | \n2325 |             try:\n2326 |                 res = res.squeeze()\n2327 |             except AttributeError:  # allow e.g., scalars and frames to pass\n2328 |                 pass\n2329 | \n2330 |             # interpret the result of the filter\n2331 |             if is_bool(res) or (is_scalar(res) and isna(res)):\n2332 |                 if notna(res) and res:\n2333 |                     indices.append(self._get_index(name))\n2334 |             else:\n2335 |                 # non scalars aren't allowed\n2336 |                 raise TypeError(\n2337 |                     f\"filter function returned a {type(res).__name__}, \"\n2338 |                     \"but expected a scalar bool\"\n2339 |                 )\n2340 | \n2341 |         return self._apply_filter(indices, dropna)\n```\n\nFound file `pandas/core/generic.py`.\nFound file `pandas/core/dtypes/generic.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me analyze the code structure to understand t