e object is unchanged.\n848 | \n849 |         This method is most useful when you don't know if your\n850 |         object is a Series or DataFrame, but you do know it has just a single\n851 |         column. In that case you can safely call `squeeze` to ensure you have a\n852 |         Series.\n853 | \n854 |         Parameters\n855 |         ----------\n856 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}, default None\n857 |             A specific axis to squeeze. By default, all length-1 axes are\n858 |             squeezed. For `Series` this parameter is unused and defaults to `None`.\n859 | \n860 |         Returns\n861 |         -------\n862 |         DataFrame, Series, or scalar\n863 |             The projection after squeezing `axis` or all the axes.\n864 | \n865 |         See Also\n866 |         --------\n867 |         Series.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting scalars.\n868 |         DataFrame.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting Series.\n869 |         Series.to_frame : Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 |         >>> df_a\n914 |            a\n915 |         0  1\n916 |         1  3\n917 | \n918 |         So the columns can be squeezed down, resulting in a Series:\n919 | \n920 |         >>> df_a.squeeze(\"columns\")\n921 |         0    1\n922 |         1    3\n923 |         Name: a, dtype: int64\n924 | \n925 |         Slicing a single row from a single column will produce a single\n926 |         scalar DataFrame:\n927 | \n928 |         >>> df_0a = df.loc[df.index < 1, [\"a\"]]\n929 |         >>> df_0a\n930 |            a\n931 |         0  1\n932 | \n933 |         Squeezing the rows produces a single scalar Series:\n934 | \n935 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze(\"rows\")\n936 |         a    1\n937 |         Name: 0, dtype: int64\n938 | \n939 |         Squeezing all axes will project directly into a scalar:\n940 | \n941 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze()\n942 |         1\n943 |         \"\"\"\n944 |         axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)\n945 |         result = self.iloc[\n946 |             tuple(\n947 |                 0 if i in axes and len(a) == 1 else slice(None)\n948 |                 for i, a in enumerate(self.axes)\n949 |             )\n950 |         ]\n951 |         if isinstance(result, NDFrame):\n952 |             result = result.__finalize__(self, method=\"squeeze\")\n953 |         return result\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"typing\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/io/parsers/c_parser_wrapper.py:CParserWrapper`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass CParserWrapper(ParserBase):\n    low_memory: bool\n    _reader: parsers.TextReader\n\n    def __init__(self, src: ReadCsvBuffer[str], **kwds) -> None:\n...\n    def close(self) -> None:\n...\n    def _set_noconvert_columns(self) -> None:\n...\n    def read(\n        self,\n        nrows: int | None = None,\n    ) -> tuple[\n        Index | MultiIndex | None,\n        Sequence[Hashable] | MultiIndex,\n        Mapping[Hashable, AnyArrayLike],\n    ]:\n\n```\nHint: Search `pandas/io/parsers/c_parser_wrapper.py:CParserWrapper` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `pandas/io/stata.py:_cast_to_stata_types`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n511 | def _cast_to_stata_types(data: DataFrame) -> DataFrame:\n512 |     \"\"\"\n513 |     Checks the dtypes of the columns of a pandas DataFrame for\n514 |     compatibility with the data types and ranges supported by Stata, and\n515 |     converts if necessary.\n516 | \n517 |     Parameters\n518 |     ----------\n519 |     data : DataFrame\n520 |         The DataFrame to check and convert\n521 | \n522 |     Notes\n523 |     -----\n524 |     Numeric columns in Stata must be one of int8, int16, int32, float32 or\n525 |     float64, with some additional value restrictions.  int8 and int16 columns\n526 |     are checked for violations of the value restrictions and upcast if needed.\n527 |     int64 data is not usable in Stata, and so it is downcast to int32 whenever\n528 |     the value are in the int32 range, and sidecast to float64 when larger than\n529 |     this range.  If the int64 values are outside of the range of those\n530 |     perfectly representable as float64 values, a warning is raised.\n531 | \n532 |     bool columns are cast to int8.  uint columns are converted to int of the\n533 |     same size if there is no loss in precision, otherwise are upcast to a\n534 |     larger type.  uint64 is currently not supported since it is concerted to\n535 |     object in a DataFrame.\n536 |     \"\"\"\n537 |     ws = \"\"\n538 |     # original, if small, if large\n539 |     conversion_data: tuple[\n540 |         tuple[type, type, type],\n541 |         tuple[type, type, type],\n542 |         tuple[type, type, type],\n543 |         tuple[type, type, type],\n544 |         tuple[type, type, type],\n545 |     ] = (\n546 |         (np.bool_, np.int8, np.int8),\n547 |         (np.uint8, np.int8, np.int16),\n548 |         (np.uint16, np.int16, np.int32),\n549 |         (np.uint32, np.int32, np.int64),\n550 |         (np.uint64, np.int64, np.float64),\n551 |     )\n552 | \n553 |     float32_max = struct.unpack(\"<f\", b\"\\xff\\xff\\xff\\x7e\")[0]\n554 |     float64_max = struct.unpack(\"<d\", b\"\\xff\\xff\\xff\\xff\\xff\\xff\\xdf\\x7f\")[0]\n555 | \n556 |     for col in data:\n557 |         # Cast from unsupported types to supported types\n558 |         is_nullable_int = (\n559 |             isinstance(data[col].dtype, ExtensionDtype)\n560 |             and data[col].dtype.kind in \"iub\"\n561 |         )\n562 |         # We need to find orig_missing before altering data below\n563 |         orig_missing = data[col].isna()\n564 |         if is_nullable_int:\n565 |             fv = 0 if data[col].dtype.kind in \"iu\" else False\n566 |             # Replace with NumPy-compatible column\n567 |             data[col] = data[col].fillna(fv).astype(data[col].dtype.numpy_dtype)\n568 |         elif isinstance(data[col].dtype, ExtensionDtype):\n569 |             if getattr(data[col].dtype, \"numpy_dtype\", None) is not None:\n570 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(data[col].dtype.numpy_dtype)\n571 |             elif is_string_dtype(data[col].dtype):\n572 |                 # TODO could avoid converting string dtype to object here,\n573 |                 # but handle string dtype in _encode_strings\n574 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(\"object\")\n575 |                 # generate_table checks for None values\n576 |                 data.loc[data[col].isna(), col] = None\n577 | \n578 |         dtype = data[col].dtype\n579 |         empty_df = data.shape[0] == 0\n580 |         for c_data in conversion_data:\n581 |             if dtype == c_data[0]:\n582 |                 if empty_df or data[col].max() <= np.iinfo(c_data[1]).max:\n583 |                     dtype = c_data[1]\n584 |                 else:\n585 |                     dtype = c_data[2]\n586 |                 if c_data[2] == np.int64:  # Warn if necessary\n587 |                     if data[col].max() >= 2**53:\n588 |                         ws = precision_loss_doc.format(\"uint64\", \"float64\")\n589 | \n590 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(dtype)\n591 | \n592 |         # Check values and upcast if necessary\n593 | \n594 |         if dtype == np.int8 and not empty_df:\n595 |             if data[col].max() > 100 or data[col].min() < -127:\n596 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(np.int16)\n597 |         elif dtype == np.int16 and not empty_df:\n598 |             if data[col].max() > 32740 or data[col].min() < -32767:\n599 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(np.int32)\n600 |         elif dtype == np.int64:\n601 |             if empty_df or (\n602 |                 data[col].max() <= 2147483620 and data[col].min() >= -2147483647\n603 |             ):\n604 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(np.int32)\n605 |             else:\n606 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(np.float64)\n607 |                 if data[col].max() >= 2**53 or data[col].min() <= -(2**53):\n608 |                     ws = precision_loss_doc.format(\"int64\", \"float64\")\n609 |         elif dtype in (np.float32, np.float64):\n610 |             if np.isinf(data[col]).any():\n611 |                 raise ValueError(\n612 |                     f\"Column {col} contains infinity or -infinity\"\n613 |                     \"which is outside the range supported by Stata.\"\n614 |                 )\n615 |             value = data[col].max()\n616 |             if dtype == np.float32 and value > float32_max:\n617 |                 data[col] = data[col].astype(np.float64)\n618 |             elif dtype == np.float64:\n619 |                 if value > float64_max:\n620 |                     raise ValueError(\n621 |                         f\"Column {col} has a maximum value ({value}) outside the range \"\n622 |                         f\"supported by Stata ({float64_max})\"\n623 |                     )\n624 |         if is_nullable_int:\n625 |             if orig_missing.any():\n626 |                 # Replace missing by Stata sentinel value\n627 |                 sentinel = StataMissingValue.BASE_MISSING_VALUES[data[col].dtype.name]\n628 |                 data.loc[orig_missing, col] = sentinel\n629 |     if ws:\n630 |         warnings.warn(\n631 |             ws,\n632 |             PossiblePrecisionLoss,\n633 |             stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n634 |         )\n635 | \n636 |     return data\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/arrays/boolean.py:BooleanDtype.type`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n77 | def type(self) -> type:  # type: ignore[override]\n78 |         return np.bool_\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/dtypes/base.py:ExtensionDtype.type`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n163 | def type(self) -> type_t[Any]:\n164 |         \"\"\"\n165 |         The scalar type for the array, e.g. ``int``\n166 | \n167 |         It's expected ``ExtensionArray[item]`` returns an instance\n168 |         of ``ExtensionDtype.type`` for scalar ``item``, assuming\n169 |         that value is valid (not NA). NA values do not need to be\n170 |         instances of `type`.\n171 |         \"\"\"\n172 |         raise AbstractMethodError(self)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:SparseDtype.type`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1898 | def type(self):\n1899 |         return self.subtype.type